WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.740
Welcome to the lecture on database implementation.

2
00:00:03.340 --> 00:00:07.600
So in the last lecture, we started looking at set-oriented query processing.

3
00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:18.900
And we said that for efficiency reasons, we prefer processing schemes where we don't look at individual data items, but instead look at large sets of items.

4
00:00:19.240 --> 00:00:23.720
Because we can process much more efficiently than if we just look at one item after the other.

5
00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.400
For example, we can use techniques like sorging, hashing, indexing, etc.

6
00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:33.220
So that pay off if you have large sets, but for a single table, of course, an index doesn't

7
00:00:33.220 --> 00:00:33.660
make sense.

8
00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:39.340
So therefore, we prefer to process large sets of data values.

9
00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:49.220
And we said, usually we do this by expressing our query processing plan or strategy as relational

10
00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:49.520
algebra.

11
00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:55.660
So here we see a typical example, if you would run a SQL query that says, find me all students

12
00:00:55.660 --> 00:01:00.780
that attend lectures and databases, this is what we get.

13
00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.940
So we would look at one set here, this set or this relation more precisely, this relation

14
00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:11.720
we join together, this gives us, at least conceptually, gives us a new relation.

15
00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:15.680
We might not store it like this, but conceptually this gives me a new relation.

16
00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:24.100
Then I have the lectures relation again, I construct a new relation by filtering on database

17
00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:26.780
title and then we join them together and then we get the answer.

18
00:01:27.260 --> 00:01:35.800
So our model here is that the operators that we have and operators are join, select, this

19
00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:40.580
is select, join, select, and this here you don't see it because we usually write the

20
00:01:40.580 --> 00:01:44.120
name of the relation, but this is called a table scan.

21
00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:48.080
So an operator that reads a table from disk or from memory.

22
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:54.500
And so in each operator consumes zero or more relations and produces one output relation.

23
00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:55.700
So this is our model here.

24
00:01:56.080 --> 00:02:00.300
And this processing model is inherently set oriented because it operates on set of data.

25
00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:06.840
Now when I say set of data, this is strictly speaking not true because SQL allows us to

26
00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:08.100
have duplicates in our input.

27
00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:11.080
If we don't have a primary key, then we can have duplicates.

28
00:02:11.500 --> 00:02:13.820
Therefore, the algebra here is back-oriented.

29
00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:15.580
So we are multi-set oriented.

30
00:02:15.800 --> 00:02:18.660
So we can have multiple copies of the same data value,

31
00:02:18.840 --> 00:02:21.160
which actually sometimes is a pain.

32
00:02:21.300 --> 00:02:23.400
Some of the problems become more difficult,

33
00:02:23.620 --> 00:02:25.220
because we can have duplicates.

34
00:02:25.300 --> 00:02:26.560
If everything would be duplicate-free,

35
00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.540
this would usually be easier.

36
00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:30.720
But SQL is not like this.

37
00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:33.780
So we must cope with the fact that we might have duplicates.

38
00:02:35.320 --> 00:02:39.080
Now what interests us in this lecture, of course, is how do we implement this?

39
00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:42.960
So how do we implement such algebraic operators?

40
00:02:43.800 --> 00:02:47.800
And later we look at how really we implement them.

41
00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:54.260
So before we look at individual operators, you must understand what are the general strategies

42
00:02:54.260 --> 00:02:54.880
for doing so.

43
00:02:55.300 --> 00:03:00.340
And now one thing you must keep in mind is that there are actually multiple points in

44
00:03:00.340 --> 00:03:01.100
time, if you wish.

45
00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:06.920
And so this here at time point T1, we compile our database system.

46
00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:18.240
Of course this is usually a long time ago, so this has been very, like weeks, months,

47
00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:20.060
years ago, that's a long time ago.

48
00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:26.240
So then sometime later, at T2, we compile the query,

49
00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:34.060
and so some SQL query, for example SQL.

50
00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:38.160
And then of course, again at some point later,

51
00:03:38.200 --> 00:03:39.780
now this is probably closer to T2,

52
00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:42.240
but can at least conceptually also be quite late,

53
00:03:42.700 --> 00:03:43.900
we execute the query.

54
00:03:47.620 --> 00:03:53.600
In particular, this separation causes quite a few challenges, but first let's look at

55
00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:55.020
the one between T1 and T2.

56
00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:59.520
One problem of course is that when we build our database systems, when we implement it,

57
00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:04.920
when we test this, we have no idea which queries will be executed later in our system.

58
00:04:05.700 --> 00:04:11.600
Because we built a database system, we often ship it to a customer, so we might run it

59
00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.580
on a cloud server instead, but nevertheless, we installed it somewhere.

60
00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.500
And then it is more or less out of our control.

61
00:04:17.900 --> 00:04:20.940
And at some later point in time, a user types in some SQL,

62
00:04:21.100 --> 00:04:22.460
and of course, then expects this works.

63
00:04:24.260 --> 00:04:26.880
So, this means that you must implement your operators in a way

64
00:04:26.880 --> 00:04:29.060
that they can cope with arbitrary queries.

65
00:04:29.220 --> 00:04:30.480
And because we don't know the queries,

66
00:04:30.580 --> 00:04:34.100
so when we implement operators, we don't know which queries will be executed.

67
00:04:34.380 --> 00:04:36.120
Therefore, we must implement them in a way,

68
00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:39.420
so that they can land everything.

69
00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:45.180
And to illustrate this, of course, we will see more examples later, but just to illustrate

70
00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:52.700
this, think about, let's say we want to implement something like set intersection.

71
00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:59.640
So we want to take two sets or two relations and now we want to find all values that occur

72
00:04:59.640 --> 00:05:01.460
in both sides, both sets.

73
00:05:01.700 --> 00:05:03.060
So regular set intersection.

74
00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:09.740
And now what we want, and of course we have no idea what this set will be like.

75
00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:14.060
Is it an integer column, is it a string column, is it two columns, we have no idea.

76
00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.260
Nevertheless we must implement our intersection in a way that always works.

77
00:05:19.440 --> 00:05:28.860
And one way to do this is to assume that we have a kind of abstract helper logic, we will

78
00:05:28.860 --> 00:05:30.740
usually call them subscripts.

79
00:05:30.740 --> 00:05:33.560
So we call subscripts because we attach them to the operator

80
00:05:33.560 --> 00:05:36.100
when we write it down.

81
00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.740
So we have a subscript and this contains

82
00:05:39.740 --> 00:05:40.860
a query specific part.

83
00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:43.760
Like so we have might have here our intersect operator

84
00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:44.460
here on top.

85
00:05:44.920 --> 00:05:46.660
So this here might be our intersect.

86
00:05:47.220 --> 00:05:48.420
I then say, okay, to an input intersect

87
00:05:48.420 --> 00:05:50.160
I need three operations.

88
00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.620
First, it must be possible to read my left input.

89
00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:55.400
So I need a function that gives me my left input.

90
00:05:56.160 --> 00:05:58.200
I need a function that gives me my right input.

91
00:05:59.800 --> 00:06:03.820
and I need a function that compares two elements from both inputs.

92
00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:13.200
And now in reality we might need more than this, but this is indeed about the smallest

93
00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:18.000
way we can interplay into intersection because if you think about this, what we can do is,

94
00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:26.180
at least one way to implement this is to say, I read my left input and then I sort my left

95
00:06:26.180 --> 00:06:30.120
input and I can do this because I have a comparison function. So I sort my left input,

96
00:06:30.520 --> 00:06:34.760
then I read my right input and again I sort my right input and I can do this with the comparison

97
00:06:34.760 --> 00:06:40.560
function. And then I scan both lists which are sorted and find all elements that occur on both

98
00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:47.720
sides. And again I can do this because of my comparison function. Now often you will not

99
00:06:47.720 --> 00:06:51.860
want to implement it like this. For example you might prefer hash tables instead of sorting,

100
00:06:52.060 --> 00:06:55.940
but then we need more subscripts. For example we might have what's written down here,

101
00:06:56.180 --> 00:06:57.040
We might need a function,

102
00:06:57.280 --> 00:06:59.600
need a way to hash a value from left or right.

103
00:06:59.680 --> 00:07:01.480
And so we need hash functions or some other stuff.

104
00:07:02.580 --> 00:07:05.260
But this is of course depends on our implementation,

105
00:07:05.560 --> 00:07:06.900
but this is about the minimum set.

106
00:07:07.100 --> 00:07:08.460
So with these three operators,

107
00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:13.260
these three subscripts, you can implement this.

108
00:07:13.820 --> 00:07:15.240
And note that this,

109
00:07:16.140 --> 00:07:19.520
how this implementation actually makes no assumption

110
00:07:19.520 --> 00:07:21.060
about the tuples at all.

111
00:07:21.880 --> 00:07:24.720
Now, if I say at all, it's not a hundred percent true.

112
00:07:26.180 --> 00:07:29.160
a large value is because if I change the sort order,

113
00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:30.500
I might, but perhaps if not,

114
00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:32.400
because I can do everything with indirection.

115
00:07:32.580 --> 00:07:35.560
And so it might be okay to not even know

116
00:07:35.560 --> 00:07:36.840
how large the data items is,

117
00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:38.940
because what I can always do is I have here,

118
00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:40.120
these are my data items.

119
00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:43.140
So just written, oh, sorry, this is here,

120
00:07:43.340 --> 00:07:45.960
A, B, C or something.

121
00:07:46.540 --> 00:07:49.220
And so we can just maintain a pointer array to all this,

122
00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:52.100
points to the first value, points to the second value,

123
00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.420
points to the third value.

124
00:07:54.040 --> 00:07:59.680
and then I can just sort the pointers using the comparison function.

125
00:07:59.780 --> 00:08:02.500
So because I look at the content and then I call the comparison function.

126
00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:05.880
So I might call this pointer values instead,

127
00:08:06.040 --> 00:08:09.720
and then I don't even have to know how large the values themselves are.

128
00:08:10.020 --> 00:08:14.160
So I can implement an intersection knowing very, very little about the input.

129
00:08:14.400 --> 00:08:15.560
Data types, doesn't matter.

130
00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:20.560
Well, size of the tuple depends on how we implement it, but might not matter either.

131
00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:24.980
We just have to know, okay, how can I get my pointers to my left side?

132
00:08:25.040 --> 00:08:26.460
How can I get my pointers to my right side?

133
00:08:26.540 --> 00:08:28.100
And how can I compare two elements?

134
00:08:28.280 --> 00:08:30.580
So this is everything that I really need to know.

135
00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:34.520
This is an interesting way to implement operators.

136
00:08:34.760 --> 00:08:38.780
Of course, of course, it's not only interesting because for database it's basically required

137
00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.060
because of the problem that we don't know what the user will do later.

138
00:08:42.220 --> 00:08:49.240
So we must implement everything in such a way so that we assume little or nearly none about our input.

139
00:08:51.180 --> 00:08:53.480
Okay, so, and if you think about this,

140
00:08:53.480 --> 00:08:55.560
what this means is that the algebraic operators

141
00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.600
defined an abstract logic for processing.

142
00:08:58.760 --> 00:08:59.880
As we say, for example,

143
00:09:00.060 --> 00:09:01.300
towards the left side towards the right side.

144
00:09:01.420 --> 00:09:03.040
So this is very abstract operations.

145
00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:06.520
And everything that is specific to the data item,

146
00:09:06.620 --> 00:09:07.860
like how do we do a comparison?

147
00:09:08.040 --> 00:09:09.620
What is the attribute that I'm interested in?

148
00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:12.680
Also, this is always hidden in these subscripts.

149
00:09:12.820 --> 00:09:15.040
So these small things that are attached to an operator,

150
00:09:15.480 --> 00:09:17.060
that tell me how everything works.

151
00:09:17.060 --> 00:09:21.460
Now in particular, usually we, the operator does not know

152
00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:24.380
the data type or the bite size of the input tuple.

153
00:09:24.540 --> 00:09:26.540
So we have no idea if it's a string or integer.

154
00:09:26.800 --> 00:09:28.920
And the interesting thing is we don't really care.

155
00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:32.320
So we don't have to know if it's a string or an integer,

156
00:09:32.600 --> 00:09:35.380
but only thing that I need is I need a way

157
00:09:35.380 --> 00:09:38.780
to compare two elements, but this I get via a subscript.

158
00:09:38.840 --> 00:09:44.060
So somebody attaches the subscript to my operator.

159
00:09:44.280 --> 00:09:46.880
And then, so I know this is what I do.

160
00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:49.040
By the way, why is the name subscript?

161
00:09:49.220 --> 00:09:50.880
Just think about this, if this here, for example,

162
00:09:51.000 --> 00:09:53.080
this is a select and if I attach here,

163
00:09:53.660 --> 00:09:56.780
this is a typical example of a subscript.

164
00:09:57.000 --> 00:09:59.020
Also the predicate that we want to apply.

165
00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:03.000
But in reality, many operators have a lot of subscripts,

166
00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:04.840
more than what we usually write down

167
00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:08.000
when we by hand draw an algebra tree.

168
00:10:09.480 --> 00:10:11.420
But if, just if you're curious,

169
00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:15.180
you can go to a database tool

170
00:10:17.220 --> 00:10:26.580
For example, if you go to our own system, you can go to either this one, our new project,

171
00:10:28.780 --> 00:10:35.800
ombradb.com, or instead you can go to our previous one, hyperdb.postwork.org.

172
00:10:36.260 --> 00:10:41.480
And so there you can enter a secret query and then you can say explain.

173
00:10:41.480 --> 00:10:51.560
as a plan, please show me the plan, this shows you the algebra plan, but then when you click on it with your mouse,

174
00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:58.900
you get more information, or if you want to see it in full beauty, so in raw form, as human friendly,

175
00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:04.600
you can affect in our these two systems, you can also in the secret query, in the box, you can just say,

176
00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:16.540
explain verbose and then select whatever you want to do, so some query.

177
00:11:17.000 --> 00:11:21.980
And now this is verbose setting here, it will mean that this is not only shows you the operator tree,

178
00:11:22.420 --> 00:11:24.260
but will indeed show you all the subscripts.

179
00:11:24.500 --> 00:11:27.900
So everything is attached to the operators now is shown to you.

180
00:11:28.040 --> 00:11:31.440
So you can see what the system uses.

181
00:11:33.300 --> 00:11:36.340
So we don't know the data type, usually we don't know the size.

182
00:11:36.900 --> 00:11:40.360
We also usually do not understand the content of a tuple.

183
00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:46.040
So am I interested in the first column, in the first attribute, in the second column

184
00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:46.980
attribute, it doesn't really matter.

185
00:11:47.280 --> 00:11:52.980
Because everything that is done with a tuple is hidden in a subscript, so operators themselves

186
00:11:52.980 --> 00:11:53.540
don't care.

187
00:11:53.960 --> 00:11:56.420
So for them the tuple is a black box we just stored.

188
00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:59.240
But the only thing the operator really cares about

189
00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:00.940
is data flow and control flow.

190
00:12:01.380 --> 00:12:04.360
So what do I read and write when and which,

191
00:12:05.180 --> 00:12:07.160
where and when, and which kind of functions

192
00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:09.340
do I have to call in which order?

193
00:12:10.180 --> 00:12:12.560
Okay, everything that is query dependent

194
00:12:13.340 --> 00:12:15.020
is delegated to the subscripts.

195
00:12:15.220 --> 00:12:17.860
So they hide everything here from you

196
00:12:17.860 --> 00:12:18.840
that's consenting query.

197
00:12:19.060 --> 00:12:21.460
And this means of course that our operator implementations

198
00:12:21.460 --> 00:12:22.460
are extremely generic.

199
00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:25.880
Yeah, you can run everything on the intersection.

200
00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.740
Because we implement it, our intersection will work all the time.

201
00:12:28.940 --> 00:12:33.080
If you intersect one column, 20,000 columns, it doesn't matter.

202
00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:36.720
Everything will work, because we implement it in a very generic fashion.

203
00:12:38.560 --> 00:12:41.100
There is a small note here at the end of the slide.

204
00:12:41.260 --> 00:12:46.020
You see sometimes, in a few cases, we want to know something about our tuple, because

205
00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:48.420
it allows us to do an easier implementation.

206
00:12:48.980 --> 00:12:53.520
For example, if you would know that my tuple has a fixed size, for example, because there

207
00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:58.320
no string column in there, the fixed size tuples, then we can sometimes implement this

208
00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:01.640
more efficiently. For example, sorting can be done more efficiently if we know that my

209
00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:09.960
elements are fixed size. So therefore we might give the system some information about tuples,

210
00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:15.700
but this is an exception. Usually we don't do this, and also we will always have an

211
00:13:15.700 --> 00:13:20.940
implementation that knows nothing, because you will always need a fully generic version,

212
00:13:20.940 --> 00:13:23.700
and fully generic usually means you know nothing about your data items.

213
00:13:23.980 --> 00:13:29.880
You might have additional implementations that use some knowledge, but your generic

214
00:13:29.880 --> 00:13:36.240
implementation here will really assume very, very little about my data that I want to store.

215
00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:44.920
Now here is an example how you can implement intersects. Now for simplicity, assume

216
00:13:44.920 --> 00:13:48.460
that my input is already sorted, because then it is particularly easy.

217
00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:52.680
So you see my example here is a coil intersect sorted

218
00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:57.340
and I give it three subscripts, namely left, right and compare.

219
00:13:57.640 --> 00:14:00.760
So left is kind of an object that gives me,

220
00:14:00.880 --> 00:14:03.220
or subscripts that gives me the next tuple from the left,

221
00:14:03.360 --> 00:14:04.800
right gives me the next tuple from the right

222
00:14:04.800 --> 00:14:06.080
and compare two values.

223
00:14:07.740 --> 00:14:09.780
So, and now we can implement it like we say,

224
00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:12.880
okay, first we say T1 is the next tuple from left.

225
00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:14.600
So we read the first tuple from the left side.

226
00:14:14.900 --> 00:14:17.600
And then we say, okay, and then N is here next.

227
00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:20.020
that I want to read is the right hand side.

228
00:14:20.240 --> 00:14:21.720
So I read one table from the left

229
00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.680
and to remember that the next table to be read

230
00:14:24.680 --> 00:14:25.640
comes from the right.

231
00:14:26.740 --> 00:14:28.420
Okay, then now we have a big loop.

232
00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:30.480
This is as long as my input is not exhausted.

233
00:14:31.380 --> 00:14:32.840
So as long as I'm not done.

234
00:14:33.120 --> 00:14:35.600
So if n is left, then I want to read from the left.

235
00:14:36.260 --> 00:14:38.960
If n is left, then I say t1 is the next table from left,

236
00:14:39.140 --> 00:14:40.860
else t2 is next table from the right.

237
00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:44.360
Okay, now I have two table pointers, t1, t2.

